From 2ca1a7d3eddad947aee1ea790a7a22e0d9d399ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 21:48:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: issue 42: __reussir_drop_run: outside a drain,
 releases a cell at once in a drain of its own (inside one it only defers it),
 instead of a deferral and a drain

A host that frees one cell through the pending stack outside a drain
(lean2rr's runtime: the last reference to a record or a boxed payload
that a set, a pop or a reference's set gives up) had to call
__reussir_drop_defer, then __reussir_drop_drain, which pops the cell
again and releases it in drain_one: about 17 + 18 + 26 instructions of
bookkeeping per freed cell, three calls.

__reussir_drop_run(cell, release) does the same in one call:

- inside a drain it defers the cell exactly as __reussir_drop_defer
  does (a narrow deferral; the running drain releases it);
- otherwise it sets draining, calls release(cell) and ends as drain_one
  does once its cell is popped: drained() if nothing is pending, else
  drain_slow.

The pending stack is last in, first out: a cell pushed on top is popped
first, and what its release pushes goes on top again. So releasing it at
once, with any pending work left where it is (work pushed outside a
drain), gives the same releases and steps in the same order, with the
same depth() and active() at each. Only the bookkeeping differs: no
entry for the cell, so a run on top is not moved to the vector and back.
The function is extern "C" like the other entry points: no unwinding,
and the drained hook is called once per run outside a drain, after it.
Unlike drain_one it does not set n to 0: nothing was popped, and a run
pending outside a drain must stay.

Tests (drop/tests.rs): a scenario action Run, done either through
__reussir_drop_run or through a deferral and a drain;
random_runs_match_defer_and_drain runs 400 random forests both ways
(roots released by a run or in line, so runs happen inside a drain,
outside one with nothing pending and outside one with work pending, all
three checked to occur) and asserts the same events with the same depth
and activity, the same calls of the drained hook (each after its drain:
not active, depth 0), and the model's order; run_outside_a_drain,
run_with_pending_work and run_inside_a_drain give hand-made forests and
their expected orders; drained_runs_once_per_run checks one hook call per
run outside a drain: nothing pushed, a cell pushed, a cell or a step
pending before, a release that pushes only a step, a run inside the
drain. The existing scenarios are unchanged: the digest of the 400
scenarios is still 5906a65ce6ae636a. cargo test -p reussir-rt (51
tests), the drop tests in release mode, and under Miri with strict
provenance (stacked and tree borrows) pass.

Review (local notes review-rtperf): correct, equivalent to a deferral
and a drain in every state. R42-01: the module comment and this subject
say that a run inside a drain only defers. R42-02: the hook checks missed
a stack with no run but a step (n == 0, len > 0); the cases with a step
pending and with a release that pushes only a step, and the hook calls
compared in the random forests, now fail for a run that tests n alone.
