+/// [`__reussir_drop_defer`] of `cell` and `release` followed by
+/// [`__reussir_drop_drain`], in one call: outside a drain the cell is
+/// released at once, instead of being pushed and then popped. `cell` is
+/// what [`__reussir_drop_defer`] takes (a box whose count was read as 1 and
+/// which nothing else references, or an immediate's dummy box); it is
+/// passed to `release` unchanged.
+///
+/// - Inside a drain (from a release or a step), the cell is deferred
+///   exactly as [`__reussir_drop_defer`] defers it: the running drain
+///   releases it.
+/// - Otherwise this call starts a drain: [`active`] is true while
+///   `release(cell)` runs, so the cells that release frees are pushed, not
+///   released in line. The drain then ends as one of
+///   [`__reussir_drop_drain`] with one cell pending does: if nothing is
+///   pending, it ends at once and calls [`__reussir_drop_drained`]'s
+///   function; else the general loop releases what is pending, last pushed
+///   first, then calls that function.
+///
+/// **Order.** The stack is last in, first out. A cell pushed on top of it
+/// is popped first and released before anything pushed earlier, and what
+/// its release pushes goes on top again. So releasing the cell at once,
+/// with the pending work left where it is, is the same as pushing it and
+/// draining, also when work is pending (pushed outside a drain): the same
+/// releases and steps in the same order, with the same [`depth`] and
+/// [`active`] at each (a pushed cell is popped before its release runs).
+/// Only the stack's own bookkeeping differs: no entry is made for the cell,
+/// so a run on top stays in place instead of going to the vector and back.
+///
+/// Like the other entry points this function is `extern "C"` and cannot
+/// unwind: `release` cannot either, and a panic in a step or in the
+/// function in [`__reussir_drop_drained`] aborts, as in any drain.
+#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
+pub unsafe extern "C" fn __reussir_drop_run(cell: *mut u8, release: Release) {
+    let state = state();
+    if state.draining.get() {
+        return unsafe { __reussir_drop_defer(cell, release) };
+    }
+    // As in `drain_one`, once its cell is popped: outside a drain nothing is
+    // popped, so `release` pops nothing either (a drain started inside it
+    // returns at once). With nothing pending after it, nothing was pending
+    // before it and nothing was pushed: the table is still empty and the
+    // vector no longer than KEEP.
+    state.draining.set(true);
+    unsafe { release(cell) };
+    if state.n.get() == 0 && state.len.get() == 0 {
+        state.draining.set(false);
+        drained();
+        return;
+    }
+    unsafe { drain_slow() }
+}
